# Routh I.Art.88 — masses in geometric progression give velocities in
# geometric progression — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 88: "A series of perfectly elastic balls are arranged in the
# same straight line, one of them impinges directly on the next and so on;
# prove that if their masses form a geometrical progression of which the
# common ratio is 2, their velocities after impact will form a geometrical
# progression."
#
# THE DERIVATION, IN FULL. For a direct elastic impact of m (speed u) on m'
# at rest, the impact equations of Arts. 83-84 give the struck ball
#
#     v' = 2*m*u / (m + m')
#
# Put m' = r*m, a geometric progression of masses with common ratio r:
#
#     v' = 2*m*u / (m*(1 + r)) = u * 2/(1 + r)
#
# The mass m has cancelled. Every ball therefore leaves at the SAME fraction
# 2/(1+r) of the speed of the ball that struck it, so the sequence of speeds
# is itself a geometric progression -- with common ratio 2/(1+r), not r. For
# Routh's r = 2 that ratio is 2/3.
#
# THE REBOUNDS DO IT TOO. The striking ball is left with
# v = u*(m - m')/(m + m') = u*(1 - r)/(1 + r), which for r = 2 is -u/3. Since
# each incoming u is 2/3 of the last, the rebound speeds form a geometric
# progression of ratio 2/3 as well -- running backwards down the line.
#
# THE NUMBERS. Four balls, masses 1, 2, 4, 8 (ratio 2), all perfectly elastic
# (restitution 1), the first arriving at unit speed. Predicted:
#
#   struck-ball speeds   1,  2/3,  4/9,  8/27      = 1, 0.6667, 0.4444, 0.2963
#   rebound speeds          -1/3, -2/9, -4/27      = -0.3333, -0.2222, -0.1481
#
# so the final state is
#
#   obj0.vx = -1/3  = -0.3333333333333333
#   obj1.vx = -2/9  = -0.2222222222222222
#   obj2.vx = -4/27 = -0.14814814814814814
#   obj3.vx =  8/27 =  0.2962962962962963
#
# Two independent checks the cells print: total momentum is
# 1(-1/3) + 2(-2/9) + 4(-4/27) + 8(8/27) = 27/27 = 1, exactly the initial
# momentum; and because every impact is perfectly elastic the run reports
# |dE/E| = 0 -- no vis viva is lost anywhere (contrast Art. 90, where any
# e < 1 loses some).
#
# SETUP NOTES. Gravity is off (g_constant = 0) -- posim's pairwise gravity is
# on by default with G = 1 and would curve the line. Balls of radius 0.4 touch
# at 0.8 centre separation, and they sit at x = 0, 2, 4, 6, so each gap takes
# 1.2 units of travel. The cascade completes by t = 5.7 and the run goes to 8.
# Exactly three collisions occur: after each impact the striking ball recoils
# more slowly than the ball behind it recoils, so no pair ever meets twice.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a pulse walking down a line of growing balls, each one
# leaving at two thirds of the speed of the last, while the struck balls drift
# backwards in the same fixed ratio.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_geometric_progression
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_geometric_progression.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_geometric_progression.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
collide on

new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.4, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [1, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 2, radius = 0.4, position = [2, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 4, radius = 0.4, position = [4, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 8, radius = 0.4, position = [6, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }

momentum
energy

run 8 steps 8

# the four final velocities: -1/3, -2/9, -4/27, +8/27
obj0.vx
obj1.vx
obj2.vx
obj3.vx
# each struck ball left at 2/3 of the previous one's arrival speed:
obj3.vx / 0.2962962962962963
system.collisions
momentum
energy
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
collide on
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.4, position = [0, 0, 0], velocity = [1, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 2, radius = 0.4, position = [2, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 4, radius = 0.4, position = [4, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 8, radius = 0.4, position = [6, 0, 0], restitution = 1 }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
